{
  "requirement": "The `trust-but-verify` crate's `outline` module provides language selection,\nsource outlining, selector resolution, and source structural hashes.\nApplications can use these operations directly on files and supplied source\nsnapshots.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement `requirements.sdk.outlines.independent` says the crate's `outline` module provides four operations: language selection, source outlining, selector resolution and source structural hashes. It also says applications can use them directly on files and on supplied source snapshots. The reviewed tests are integration tests in `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/outline_content.rs`. They import only `trust_but_verify::outline` and `trust_but_verify::domain::NodeContents`, so they use the public API.\n\n- **Language selection:** `file_constructors_infer_the_language_and_return_addressed_contents` writes `AUTH.RS` with an upper-case extension and checks that the language is inferred. It asserts `fingerprint_scheme() == \"rust-structural-v1\"`. `bundled_languages_claim_extensions_and_supply_the_same_public_outline` covers every bundled language. For each one it checks the claimed extensions, the case-insensitive filename match, the structural scheme and the selector. It also checks that `PlainText` claims no extensions.\n- **Source outlining:** the same file test asserts the outline selectors are exactly `[\"\", \"fn[check_token]\"]`. `selected_contents_outlive_handles_and_later_file_edits` checks `outline().items.len() == 3` for an unknown-extension file, which falls back to plain text.\n- **Selector resolution:** the file test resolves `fn[check_token]` through `from_id` and through `select` on both the file content and the supplied-source content. It asserts the id, the text and the fingerprints. `selected_contents_outlive_handles_and_later_file_edits` resolves a selector with `from_id` and `select`. It also resolves the root selector `\"\"` through `select`.\n- **Structural hashes:** the tests assert `fingerprint()`, `structural_hash()` and the `fingerprint().scheme` format. The fingerprints of file-derived and supplied-source content are equal. The fingerprint stays unchanged after the handle is dropped and the file is edited.\n- **Files and supplied snapshots:** `from_path` and `from_id` cover files. `from_source` covers supplied source. The file test asserts both give equal root fingerprints and equal selected fingerprints. `selected_contents_outlive_handles_and_later_file_edits` shows that a selected snapshot keeps its text and fingerprint after the file changes. It also shows that a fresh `from_id` fails after the file is rewritten as `changed\\n`, which has no third item.\n\nThe requirement is a general statement of capability with no separate failure modes, so I found no unasserted obligation. All three tests carry `{test}` references to the subject. I found no missing markers or unacknowledged gaps.",
  "references": "78ca978244e2c786214b03a4a7b5167bf3ee1e90e18026fc67d7299357660038",
